WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.953
This one is a secret weapon.

2
00:00:02.953 --> 00:00:05.907
Sometimes x and y only ever appear together,

3
00:00:05.907 --> 00:00:11.321
bundled into one straight-line combination. A x, plus B y, plus C.

4
00:00:12.041 --> 00:00:14.825
When that happens, stop seeing two variables.

5
00:00:14.825 --> 00:00:17.659
Treat the whole bundle as one new variable, u.

6
00:00:17.659 --> 00:00:19.747
And the equation becomes separable.

7
00:00:23.447 --> 00:00:25.804
Two examples from Lecture 5.

8
00:00:25.804 --> 00:00:30.719
dy/dx equals x plus y, squared. The bundle is x plus y.

9
00:00:31.139 --> 00:00:36.060
And dy/dx equals two, plus e to the two x minus y plus one.

10
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:41.121
The bundle is hiding in the exponent: two x minus y plus one.

11
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.148
Compare: x plus y squared, with only y squared, is not a linear bundle.

12
00:00:46.148 --> 00:00:48.401
The substitution will not help there.

13
00:00:52.101 --> 00:00:56.551
Here is the substitution. Let u equal A x, plus B y, plus C.

14
00:00:56.971 --> 00:00:59.659
Differentiate with respect to x.

15
00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:04.760
du/dx equals A, plus B times dy/dx. Do not forget the B.

16
00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:10.839
Replace dy/dx with f of u, and you get du/dx equals A plus B f of u.

17
00:01:10.839 --> 00:01:12.916
Only u and x are left.

18
00:01:13.337 --> 00:01:17.411
That is separable. du over A plus B f of u, equals dx.

19
00:01:17.411 --> 00:01:20.579
Integrate, then put the bundle back in for u.

20
00:01:20.999 --> 00:01:26.227
For example a, u is x plus y, so u prime is one plus u squared.

21
00:01:26.227 --> 00:01:32.059
Arc tangent u equals x plus C, so y equals tangent of x plus C, minus x.

22
00:01:35.958 --> 00:01:38.478
Now Example 4 from Lecture 5.

23
00:01:38.478 --> 00:01:43.519
dy/dx equals negative two x plus y, squared, minus seven.

24
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.642
Let u equal negative two x plus y.

25
00:01:46.642 --> 00:01:52.508
Then u prime is negative two plus y prime, so y prime equals u prime plus two.

26
00:01:52.927 --> 00:01:56.976
Substitute. u prime plus two equals u squared minus seven.

27
00:01:56.976 --> 00:01:59.563
So u prime equals u squared minus nine.

28
00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:03.539
Before dividing, find the constant solutions.

29
00:02:03.539 --> 00:02:08.745
u squared minus nine is zero at u equals three and u equals negative three.

30
00:02:08.745 --> 00:02:14.396
Those are the lines y equals two x plus three, and y equals two x minus three.

31
00:02:14.816 --> 00:02:20.479
Now separate, and use partial fractions, just like the y squared minus four example.

32
00:02:20.479 --> 00:02:25.399
You get, u minus three over u plus three, equals c, e to the six x.

33
00:02:25.819 --> 00:02:30.495
Solve for u, then put back u equals negative two x plus y.

34
00:02:30.495 --> 00:02:34.019
y equals two x, plus three times one plus c e to

35
00:02:34.019 --> 00:02:37.679
the six x, over one minus c e to the six x.

36
00:02:38.098 --> 00:02:40.357
Check the constant solutions.

37
00:02:40.357 --> 00:02:43.683
c equals zero gives y equals two x plus three.

38
00:02:43.683 --> 00:02:49.520
But y equals two x minus three is not in the family, so it is a singular solution.

39
00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:54.779
Here is the picture. Two straight-line solutions, and every other solution curve

40
00:02:54.779 --> 00:02:58.076
is squeezed between them, or runs away beyond them.

41
00:03:02.377 --> 00:03:03.260
Three traps.

42
00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.759
One. Forgetting B when you differentiate.

43
00:03:06.759 --> 00:03:11.517
If u is two x minus three y, then u prime is two, minus three y prime.

44
00:03:11.936 --> 00:03:16.634
Two. Expanding the bundle into a giant polynomial. Keep it packaged as u.

45
00:03:16.634 --> 00:03:18.183
That is the whole point.

46
00:03:18.603 --> 00:03:21.264
Three. Forgetting to put the bundle back, and

47
00:03:21.264 --> 00:03:24.021
forgetting the constant solutions you divided away.

48
00:03:27.821 --> 00:03:29.085
Here is the recipe.

49
00:03:29.505 --> 00:03:31.127
One. Spot the linear bundle.

50
00:03:31.547 --> 00:03:34.852
Two. Let u be the bundle, and differentiate. Keep the B.

51
00:03:35.273 --> 00:03:39.056
Three. You get u prime equals A plus B f of u. Separate it.

52
00:03:39.475 --> 00:03:42.760
Four. Put the bundle back, and check the constant solutions.

53
00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.768
Next up, second-order equations with constant

54
00:03:45.768 --> 00:03:48.515
coefficients, and the auxiliary equation.
